<!DOCTYPE qhelp PUBLIC
  "-//Semmle//qhelp//EN"
  "qhelp.dtd">
<qhelp>
<overview>
<p>
An HTTP server started with <code>http.ListenAndServe</code>, <code>http.ListenAndServeTLS</code>,
<code>http.Serve</code> or <code>http.ServeTLS</code> uses a default <code>http.Server</code> whose
<code>ReadHeaderTimeout</code>, <code>ReadTimeout</code>, <code>WriteTimeout</code> and
<code>IdleTimeout</code> are all zero. The same is true of an explicit <code>http.Server</code> that
leaves these fields unset. A remote client can then open many connections and send request headers
or bodies very slowly (a "Slowloris" attack), tying up goroutines and file descriptors until
legitimate clients can no longer be served.
</p>
<p>
Similarly, the default HTTP client (<code>http.Get</code>, <code>http.Post</code>,
<code>http.DefaultClient</code>, ...) has no timeout. Reading the whole body of a response from a
non-constant URL with <code>io.ReadAll</code> lets the remote server stream indefinitely or send a
very large body, exhausting memory and blocking the calling goroutine.
</p>
</overview>

<recommendation>
<p>
Start servers through an explicit <code>http.Server</code> that sets at least
<code>ReadHeaderTimeout</code> (or <code>ReadTimeout</code>), and preferably also
<code>WriteTimeout</code> and <code>IdleTimeout</code>. Fetch remote content with an
<code>http.Client</code> that has a <code>Timeout</code>, and bound the amount of data read, for
example with <code>io.LimitReader</code>.
</p>
</recommendation>

<example>
<p>
The following example starts a server with no timeouts, and reads an unbounded response body
fetched with the default client:
</p>
<sample src="FileUploadResourceHandlingBad.go" />
<p>
The following example configures server timeouts, uses a client with a timeout and limits the
size of the response body that is read:
</p>
<sample src="FileUploadResourceHandlingGood.go" />
</example>

